---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";

const treeCode = `const top = chart({
  mark: "bar",
  data: totals,
  encoding: { x: "id", y: "value" },
});

const matrix = chart({
  mark: "matrix",
  data: cells,
  encoding: { x: "id", group: "set", y: "active" },
});

const body = stackX([leftBars, matrix, detailChart]);
const view = stackY([top, body], { align: [matrix, matrix] });`;

const embedCode = `const events = repeat(eventRows, (event) =>
  chart({
    mark: "pie",
    data: event.parts,
    encoding: { x: "category", y: "value" },
  }),
);

const sequence = embed(
  sequenceContainer({ xDomain: weeks, yDomain: learners }),
  events,
  { x: "week", y: "learner", key: "id", width: 32, height: 32 },
);`;
---

<DocsLayout title="Core Concepts | composite.js">
  <div class="docs-content">
    <header class="docs-hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">Concepts</p>
      <h1>The layout tree</h1>
      <p>
        composite.js uses one layout-node contract for charts and compositions.
        That shared contract lets you render one chart, stack several charts, or
        treat the result as a child inside a larger view.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2>Layout nodes</h2>
      <p>
        A layout node is anything created by the public factory helpers. Chart
        leaves come from <code>chart()</code>, annotations come from
        <code>text()</code>, framed groups come from <code>frame()</code>, and
        composition nodes come from stack, repeat, and embed helpers.
      </p>
      <p>
        Composition helpers expect layout nodes. Wrap chart configuration with
        <code>chart(&#123;...&#125;)</code> before passing it into a stack or
        repeat.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2>Chart leaves</h2>
      <p>
        A chart leaf owns a mark, its data rows, and an encoding object. Encoding
        values are field names from your rows, while keys such as <code>x</code>,
        <code>y</code>, <code>group</code>, and <code>size</code> describe
        visual channels.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2>Recursive composition</h2>
      <p>
        <code>stackX()</code> arranges nodes left to right and
        <code>stackY()</code> arranges nodes top to bottom. Because stacks are
        nodes too, a stack can contain charts, repeats, framed nodes, or other
        stacks.
      </p>
      <pre class="docs-code"><code>{treeCode}</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2>Repeats and embedding</h2>
      <p>
        <code>repeatX()</code> and <code>repeatY()</code> create small multiples
        along one axis. Use directionless <code>repeat()</code> with
        <code>embed()</code> when repeated children should be placed into slots
        from a container such as <code>sequenceContainer()</code>.
      </p>
      <pre class="docs-code"><code>{embedCode}</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section">
      <h2>Alignment basics</h2>
      <p>
        Stack alignment lets a child align against a nested node instead of the
        child as a whole. In an UpSet-style layout, for example, a top bar chart
        can align to the matrix inside the row beneath it while the full row
        still includes side charts.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
